        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Silicate Minerals",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "```\n+------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|              SILICATE CLASSIFICATION BY CONNECTIVITY             |\n|                                                                  |\n|  Building block: SiO₄ tetrahedron                                |\n|  Si at center, 4 oxygens at corners                              |\n|  When tetrahedra share oxygens, they polymerize                  |\n|                                                                  |\n|  ISOLATED          No sharing    Nesosilicates    Olivine        |\n|  PAIRS             1 shared O    Sorosilicates    Epidote        |\n|  RINGS             2 shared O    Cyclosilicates   Tourmaline     |\n|  SINGLE CHAIN      2 shared O    Inosilicates     Pyroxene       |\n|  DOUBLE CHAIN      2½ shared O   Inosilicates     Amphibole      |\n|  SHEETS            3 shared O    Phyllosilicates  Mica, clay     |\n|  3D FRAMEWORK      4 shared O    Tectosilicates   Quartz, feld.  |\n+------------------------------------------------------------------+"
    },
    {
      "id": "03-SILICATES.md#003",
      "path": [
        "03-SILICATES.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[proof-backfill:mineralogy:03-silicates, git-history:mineralogy:03-silicates]",
        "canonical_path": "mineralogy/03-SILICATES.md",
        "concepts": "[silicates]",
        "current_path": "mineralogy/03-SILICATES.md",
        "id": "maxim:mineralogy:silicates",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "mineralogy",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[silicates]",
        "section": "mineralogy",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Silicate Minerals",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "This is literally a graph connectivity problem:\n  0 shared corners per tetrahedron → isolated (degree 0)\n  1 shared corner → pair (degree 1 at the bridge)\n  2 shared corners → chain (degree 2)\n  3 shared corners → sheet (degree 3)\n  4 shared corners → framework (degree 4)\n```"
    },
    {
      "id": "03-SILICATES.md#004",
      "path": [
        "03-SILICATES.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[proof-backfill:mineralogy:03-silicates, git-history:mineralogy:03-silicates]",
        "canonical_path": "mineralogy/03-SILICATES.md",
        "concepts": "[silicates]",
        "current_path": "mineralogy/03-SILICATES.md",
        "id": "maxim:mineralogy:silicates",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "mineralogy",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[silicates]",
        "section": "mineralogy",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Silicate Minerals",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "The Si:O ratio drops systematically as connectivity increases: isolated (1:4) → framework (1:2). The more shared oxygens, the higher the Si/O ratio, the more polymerized, the more resistant to chemical weathering."
    },
